  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>I worked on backend systems and infrastructure for a game project. The work involved turning product and gameplay needs into services that could be changed, deployed and operated in production.</p>

  <h2>The engineering question</h2>
  <p>The useful question was not simply what to build. It was where system boundaries belonged, how changes could be delivered safely and how the people operating the software would understand what was happening after release.</p>
  <p>That changes the shape of the work. A backend is not only an API surface: it needs clear responsibilities, useful operational signals and a delivery approach that lets the team learn without making every change risky.</p>

  <h2>My contribution</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Worked on backend components and infrastructure decisions alongside the wider product and game teams.</li>
    <li>Approached changes as production work, considering deployment, observability and the likely next iteration.</li>
    <li>Helped translate evolving requirements into technical boundaries that the system could sustain.</li>
  </ul>

  <aside class="article-callout">
    <p><strong>Confidentiality matters.</strong> This account is intentionally focused on the engineering approach and scope of work. It does not publish internal architecture, operational data or customer information.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>What this illustrates</h2>
  <p>Reliable backend work is a product discipline as much as an implementation task. The best choices make it easier for a team to understand the system, release work in smaller steps and keep responsibility clear as the product evolves.</p>

